window.SLIDE_SOURCES=[{"id": "d1", "file": "1_2.1_-_Developments_in_East_Asia.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1&2.1", "Developments in East Asia", "from c. 1200 to c. 1450", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}], "id": "d1:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did Song China maintain political stability and become the economic center of Afro-Eurasian trade during the post-classical era?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}], "id": "d1:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["Following the earlier Tang Dynasty (618–907), China remained politically influential and culturally unified under the Song Dynasty (960–1279).", "Like the Tang, Song rulers relied on Confucian traditions and a centralized bureaucracy with a ruling emperor to maintain political authority across a large empire.", "Chinese emperors were traditionally believed to rule with the Mandate of Heaven which justified imperial authority as long as rulers governed effectively.", "Also used by the Tang, the civil service examination system expanded during the Song period, selecting government officials based on merit rather than family background.", "This system produced an educated class of scholar-officials who administered the government.", "The Song Dynasty"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 152149, "width": 223, "height": 321, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image2.webp"}], "id": "d1:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["Confucian ideas shaped social and political life throughout China.", "Confucian teachings emphasized hierarchy, social harmony, and filial piety, reinforcing loyalty to family and state.", "During the Song period, scholars developed Neo-Confucianism, which blended traditional Confucian ideas with influences from Buddhism and Daoism.", "In Song China, practices such as foot binding among elite women reflected Confucian ideals that emphasized female obedience and reinforced patriarchal social hierarchies.", "Confucian Society"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 30302, "width": 1024, "height": 762, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d1-image3.webp"}], "id": "d1:4"}, {"number": 5, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image4.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image4.jpeg", "bytes": 541442, "width": 1000, "height": 719, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image4.webp"}], "id": "d1:5", "visualDescription": "Map comparing Sui (589–618), Tang (618–907), and Song (960–1279) extents, with the Great Wall and Grand Canal."}, {"number": 6, "text": ["Chinese cultural traditions strongly influenced neighboring societies in East Asia.", "Countries such as Korea, Japan, and Vietnam adopted elements of Chinese political organization, writing systems, and cultural practices.", "Confucian ideas shaped government systems and education throughout the region, emphasizing hierarchy, respect for authority, and social harmony.", "In Japan, political power was decentralized under a feudal system led by the shogun and samurai class. Although an emperor still existed, most power was held by regional lords who controlled land and local military forces.", "These societies adapted Chinese traditions while maintaining their own local political structures and cultural identities.", "Chinese Traditions"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image5.png", "original": "ppt/media/image5.png", "bytes": 1189948, "width": 934, "height": 989, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image5.webp"}], "id": "d1:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["Buddhism continued to shape Asian societies in the 13th century, evolving into", "diverse branches, schools, and practices that reflected the region’s cultural richness", "Mahayana Buddhism spread across East Asia, emphasizing compassion and the pathto Enlightenment, while Theravada Buddhism remained influential in Southeast Asia.", "In China, Chan Buddhism blended with Daoist traditions, appealing to scholars and emphasizing meditation and mindfulness.", "This later spread to other parts of East Asia such as Japan where it developed into Zen Buddhism", "This blending of different religious traditions is known as syncretism.", "Spread of Buddhism"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image6.png", "original": "ppt/media/image6.png", "bytes": 1178491, "width": 670, "height": 708, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image6.webp"}], "id": "d1:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["The Song Dynasty experienced significant economic expansion.", "China developed a highly commercialized economy supported by agriculture, manufacturing, and trade.", "Urban centers such as Hangzhou and Kaifeng became major centers of commerce and population reflecting rapid urbanization driven by trade and manufacturing.", "Markets expanded as merchants traded goods throughout China and across long-distance trade networks.", "The government issued paper money, which made commercial transactions easier.", "The Grand Canal, which linked northern and southern China, facilitated", "the efficient movement of grain and goods, bolstering trade and economic unity", "Commercialized Economy"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image7.png", "original": "ppt/media/image7.png", "bytes": 88231, "width": 800, "height": 1178, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image7.webp"}], "id": "d1:8"}, {"number": 9, "text": ["Agricultural innovations helped support China’s growing population.", "The introduction of Champa rice from Vietnam allowed farmers to produce multiple harvests each year resulting in a massive increase in population. More food = more people = more cities and trade.", "Improved irrigation and farming techniques increased agricultural productivity.", "Chinese shipbuilders developed large ocean-going vessels known as junks, which used advanced sails and watertight compartments to support long-distance maritime trade.", "Chinese inventors also developed technologies such as the compass, gunpowder, papermaking, and printing.", "Technological and Agricultural Innovations"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image8.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image8.jpeg", "bytes": 39196, "width": 501, "height": 340, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image8.webp"}], "id": "d1:9"}, {"number": 10, "text": ["China played a central role in Afro-Eurasian trade networks.", "Because overland travel was slow, dangerous, and expensive, merchants primarily traded luxury goods with high value. China exported luxuries such as silk, porcelain, and tea.", "Merchants exchanged Chinese products for goods from Central Asia, the Middle East, and Europe.", "Technologies and ideas also traveled along these routes between different societies. For example, Chinese inventions such as paper and gunpowder spread westward through trade networks. These inventions would go on to inspire intellectual reforms and military techniques all over Afroeurasia.", "China and the Silk Road"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image9.png", "original": "ppt/media/image9.png", "bytes": 643000, "width": 1043, "height": 686, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image9.webp"}], "id": "d1:10"}, {"number": 11, "text": ["After the Song fell to the Mongol Yuan Dynasty, the Ming Dynasty (1368–1644) reunited China under native rule.", "Between 1405 and 1433, Admiral Zheng He led massive naval expeditions across the Indian Ocean. His fleet included hundreds of junks and traveled as far as East Africa.", "Unlike Song-era trade (driven by private merchants), Zheng He's voyages were state-funded and aimed to establish diplomatic relationships, collect tribute, and project Chinese power.", "The expeditions brought back exotic goods (such as giraffes) and strengthened China's connections with India, the Middle East, and Africa.", "The Ming Dynasty"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d1-image10.GIF", "original": "ppt/media/image10.GIF", "bytes": 141258, "width": 1600, "height": 965, "format": "GIF", "display": "assets/web/d1-image10.webp"}], "id": "d1:11"}, {"number": 12, "text": ["Song China became one of the most economically advanced societies in the world during this period.", "Technological innovation, agricultural productivity, and strong government institutions supported economic growth.", "China’s goods and technologies influenced societies across Eurasia.", "As a result, East Asia played a major role in shaping long-distance trade and cultural exchange.", "Why this matters"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d1-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 391744, "width": 1200, "height": 887, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d1-image1.webp"}], "id": "d1:12"}], "sha256": "e221dc27f4fbf928837ec5e3312cf9aad4587d07417345898f16c70d86080447", "label": "East Asia", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d2", "file": "1_2.2_-_Developments_in_Dar_al-Islam.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["© Morgan AP Teaching", "PowerPoint 1.2", "Developments in Dar al-Islam", "from c. 1200 to c. 1450", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}], "id": "d2:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did Islamic societies remain politically and culturally influential after the fragmentation of the Abbasid Caliphate?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}], "id": "d2:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["The Abbasid Caliphate (750–1258) once ruled a vast Islamic empire stretching across the Middle East, North Africa, and parts of Central Asia.", "The Abbasids established their capital at Baghdad, which became a major center of trade, culture, and learning.", "This period is often referred to as the Islamic Golden Age, when scholars made advances in mathematics, astronomy, medicine, and philosophy.", "Although the Abbasid political system eventually weakened, Islamic religion, culture, and trade networks continued to shape societies across Afro-Eurasia.", "Context"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 761992, "width": 949, "height": 509, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image2.webp"}], "id": "d2:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["During the post-classical period, the political unity of the Abbasid Caliphate declined.", "New Islamic states emerged across the region, many ruled by Turkic peoples.", "Examples included the Seljuk Empire (Persia), the Delhi Sultanate (India), and the Mamluk Sultanate (Egypt).", "These states maintained Islamic traditions while adapting to local political and cultural conditions.", "Despite political fragmentation, the Islamic world remained connected through shared religion, language, and trade networks.", "Fragmentation of the Abbasid"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 14810, "width": 474, "height": 299, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d2-image3.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image4.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image4.jpeg", "bytes": 46845, "width": 735, "height": 594, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image4.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image5.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image5.jpeg", "bytes": 201218, "width": 2000, "height": 1700, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image5.webp"}], "id": "d2:4"}, {"number": 5, "text": ["Islam continued to shape societies across North Africa, Southwest Asia, and parts of Central and South Asia.", "Islamic law (Sharia) and the Arabic language helped unify diverse societies within Dar al-Islam (the “House of Islam,” referring to regions where Islam was widely practiced).", "Mosques, religious scholars, and educational institutions played important roles in social and political life.", "Islam often blended with local traditions as it spread into new regions. For example,", "Sufi missionaries spread Islam through trade networks and cultural interaction.", "Social life in Islamic societies was also shaped by religious traditions. While Islamic law reinforced patriarchal gender roles, women could hold property, inherit wealth, and participate in economic activities such as trade.", "New Islamic States"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image6.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image6.jpeg", "bytes": 154459, "width": 1280, "height": 720, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image6.webp"}], "id": "d2:5"}, {"number": 6, "text": ["Islam expanded beyond its original heartland through trade, migration, conquest, and missionary activity.", "Muslim merchants carried Islamic beliefs along major trade routes including the Silk Roads and Indian Ocean networks.", "Sufi missionaries helped spread Islam by adapting Islamic teachingsto local cultures, a process of religious syncretism.", "Islam also spread through military expansion, such as the establishment of the Delhi Sultanate in northern India, which brought Islamic political rule to the region.", "As a result, Islam gradually spread to regions such as Southeast Asia and sub-Saharan Africa.", "This expansion created a broad cultural and religious network connecting societies across Afro-Eurasia.", "Islam’s Growth"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image7.png", "original": "ppt/media/image7.png", "bytes": 1340862, "width": 804, "height": 940, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image7.webp"}], "id": "d2:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["Islamic societies played a central role in long-distance trade across Eurasia.", "Merchants traveling through the Middle East and Central Asia traded luxury goods such as Persian rugs and textiles, spices, and preciousmetals, connecting markets from East Asia to the Mediterranean.", "Merchants often traveled in caravans, large groups of traders, animals,and goods moving together for safety across long desert routes.", "Trade routes were supported by roadside inns known as caravanserai, which provided lodging, food, and protection for merchants and their animals while also serving as places where merchants exchanged goods, news, and cultural ideas.", "Major trading cities such as Baghdad, Samarkand, and Kashgar flourished as hubs of commerce and cultural exchange.", "These networks facilitated the movement of goods, ideas, religions, and technologies across Afro-Eurasia.", "Silk Road & Caravan Trade"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image8.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image8.jpeg", "bytes": 107320, "width": 512, "height": 371, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image8.webp"}], "id": "d2:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["Scholars preserved and expanded knowledge from Greek, Roman,Indian, and Persian traditions.", "Important developments occurred in fields such as mathematics, astronomy, and medicine. The Arabic numeral system (which includes the concept of zero), developed by scholars in the Islamic world, became the standard for global trade and is still used today", "The astrolabe was invented in the Islamic world and allowed sailors to determine latitude using the stars, which enabled safer and more accurate maritime trade across open oceans like the Indian Ocean.", "Institutions like the House of Wisdom in Baghdad helped translate classical texts and promote learning.", "For example the mathematician Al-Khwarizmi developed foundational concepts in algebra.", "These achievements later influenced intellectual developments in Europe.", "Intellectual Innovations"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image9.png", "original": "ppt/media/image9.png", "bytes": 547340, "width": 562, "height": 535, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image9.webp"}], "id": "d2:8"}, {"number": 9, "text": ["Because Islamic societies were connected through trade networks, ideas and technologies moved easily across regions.", "Merchants, scholars, and travelers facilitated cultural exchangeacross Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, and South Asia.", "Travelers such as Ibn Battuta documented their journeys across the Islamic world, describing shared religious traditions and cultural connections.", "His travels demonstrate the interconnected nature of societies within Dar al-Islam (The House of Islam).", "Cultural Exchange"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image10.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image10.webp", "bytes": 42926, "width": 474, "height": 329, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d2-image10.webp"}], "id": "d2:9"}, {"number": 10, "text": ["Even after the decline of the Abbasid Caliphate, the Dar al-Islam remained one of the most interconnected regions of Afro-Eurasia.", "Shared religious traditions, the Arabic language, and Islamic law helped create a common cultural and intellectual world that stretched across North Africa, the Middle East, Central Asia, and parts of South and Southeast Asia.", "Participation in this network offered important advantages. Converting to Islam could provide greater access to trade networks, government positions, education, and social mobility within Islamic societies.", "As a result, the Dar al-Islam played a major role in facilitating economic exchange, cultural interaction, and the spread of knowledge across Afro-Eurasia.", "Why this matters", "Dar al-Islam(The House of Islam)"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d2-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 863425, "width": 1783, "height": 1235, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d2-image11.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image11.jpeg", "bytes": 151218, "width": 1650, "height": 942, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d2-image11.webp"}], "id": "d2:10"}], "sha256": "cbf77fe3177e0a2642e552e775b9f856b6bc0ceeba6e6d31e14dfd8d3ed41bb8", "label": "Dar al-Islam", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d3", "file": "1_2.3_-_Developments_in_South_and_Southeast_Asia.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1.3", "Developments in South and", "Southeast Asia c. 1200 to c. 1450", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}], "id": "d3:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did Hinduism, Islam, and Buddhism shape state formation, cultural exchange, and environmental change in South and Southeast Asia between 1200 and 1450?", "Driving Questions"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}], "id": "d3:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["From 1200 to 1450, Hinduism, Islam, and Buddhism played central roles in shaping the cultural, social, and political landscapes of the region.", "Hinduism provided a framework for social structure through the caste system and influenced art, architecture, and religious rituals in India", "Islam introduced new cultural and social practices, often blending with local traditions, particularly in regions like the Delhi Sultanate", "Buddhism continued to shape societies, with Theravada Buddhism prominent in Southeast Asia and Mahayana traditions influencing art and philosophy in places like Java and Cambodia", "Religion in South and Southeast Asia"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 341585, "width": 2500, "height": 1407, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image2.webp"}], "id": "d3:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["Hindu states, like the Vijayanagara Empire in southern India, preserved", "Hindu traditions while fostering trade and military strength. Vijayanagara", "was a centralized state with a powerful monarchy and bureaucratic administration. Rulers claimed divine right through their associationwith Hindu gods", "Buddhist states in Southeast Asia, such as the Khmer Empire, combined", "Buddhist principles with centralized authority to create enduring political", "structures. The Khmer Empire was a highly centralized state ruled by a", "divine king, believed to embody Hindu and Buddhist divine qualities", "Hindu-Buddhist states, like the Majapahit Empire (Java, c. 1293–1527), blended both religious traditions. Majapahit was a centralized state andIts rulers claimed divine authority rooted in both Hindu and Buddhist traditions. This is a clear example of syncretism, the blending of different religious and cultural traditions.\"", "These states adapted to local contexts, blending religious and cultural traditions to legitimize their rule and unify diverse populations", "Southeast Asian States"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image3.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image3.jpeg", "bytes": 13389, "width": 275, "height": 295, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image3.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 1832364, "width": 1575, "height": 1414, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image4.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image5.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image5.jpeg", "bytes": 100300, "width": 1000, "height": 675, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image5.webp"}], "id": "d3:4"}, {"number": 5, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image6.png", "original": "ppt/media/image6.png", "bytes": 2914801, "width": 2796, "height": 1885, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image6.webp"}], "id": "d3:5", "visualDescription": "Map labeled Majapahit Empire, with modern country labels and current international boundaries."}, {"number": 6, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image7.png", "original": "ppt/media/image7.png", "bytes": 2225435, "width": 1230, "height": 1586, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image7.webp"}], "id": "d3:6", "visualDescription": "Map labeled Khmer Empire, with China (Song Dynasty), Dai Viet, Burma, and Champa around it."}, {"number": 7, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image8.png", "original": "ppt/media/image8.png", "bytes": 782100, "width": 1607, "height": 1851, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image8.webp"}], "id": "d3:7", "visualDescription": "Map of southern India labeling Vijayanagara and its capital, Bahmani Kingdom, and Gajapatis."}, {"number": 8, "text": ["The spread of Islam brought new cultural and social dynamics to South and Southeast Asia, mixing with existing traditions.", "The Delhi Sultanate introduced Islamic governance and culture to northern India, fostering syncretic traditions in art, architecture, and social customs.", "Coastal trade networks facilitated the spread of Islam to Southeast Asia, where Muslim merchants and Sufi missionaries played key roles in conversion", "Islam often coexisted with Hindu and Buddhist traditions, creating diverse societies where religious practices blended and evolved", "Spread of Islam"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image9.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image9.jpeg", "bytes": 40271, "width": 571, "height": 444, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image9.webp"}], "id": "d3:8"}, {"number": 9, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image10.png", "original": "ppt/media/image10.png", "bytes": 2754127, "width": 1474, "height": 1651, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image10.webp"}], "id": "d3:9", "visualDescription": "Delhi Sultanate map with extents marked 1236, 1335, and 1525, plus Tamerlane’s invasion in 1398–1399."}, {"number": 10, "text": ["Hinduism and Buddhism inspired monumental architecture and art, serving as symbols of state power and religious devotion", "Angkor Wat (Cambodia) – Initially built as a Hindu temple in the early 12th century, it later transformed into a Buddhist site. Its massive scale and intricate carvings demonstrated the Khmer king's divine authority and unified the empire", "Borobudur (Java) – A massive Buddhist temple built in the 9th century (though still culturally significant through 1200–1450). Its nine stacked platforms represent the Buddhist path to enlightenment and served as a pilgrimage site", "Virupaksha Temple (Vijayanagara Empire) – An active Hindu temple complex that the Vijayanagara rulers expanded and patronized to legitimize their rule, promote Hindu unity, and resist northern Islamic sultanates", "Great Mosque of Demak (Java, Indonesia) – The oldest mosque in Java, built in the late 15th century. Its architecture blends Javanese Hindu-Buddhist and Islamic styles, reflecting the peaceful integration of Islam into existing Southeast Asian cultures", "Hindu-Buddhist Influence"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image11.png", "original": "ppt/media/image11.png", "bytes": 1228783, "width": 916, "height": 643, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image11.webp"}], "id": "d3:10"}, {"number": 11, "text": ["Between 1200 and 1450, improved transportation technologies and environmental knowledge expanded the Indian Ocean trade network, fostering the rise of powerful trading cities across the region.", "The development of port cities like Malacca (Malaysia)", "and Calicut (India) facilitated the exchange of goods,", "cultures, and ideas across the Indian Ocean region", "The Indian Ocean network connected East Africa,", "South Asia, Southeast Asia, and the Middle East,", "fostering interregional exchange", "Unlike the Silk Roads (which carried mostly luxury goods),", "Indian Ocean ships could transport both luxury goods (spices,", "fine textiles) and bulk goods (timber, rice, cotton cloth)", "due to much lower shipping costs and larger ships.", "The Indian Ocean", "Calicut", "Malacca", "Monsoon Winds", "Monsoon Winds"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image12.png", "original": "ppt/media/image12.png", "bytes": 302605, "width": 1024, "height": 855, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image12.webp"}], "id": "d3:11"}, {"number": 12, "text": ["New ship technologies and environmental knowledge allowed merchants to travel farther and carry more goods than ever before.", "New ship technologies such as the dhow and junk ship with their lateen sails enabled merchants to carry larger cargoes across vast distances, increasing the volume and efficiencyof trade", "The success of Indian Ocean trade depended on advanced understanding of monsoon wind patterns – merchants timed seasonal voyages to take advantage of predictable winds that alternated between northeast and southwest directions", "The Sultanate of Malacca grew into a powerful maritime empire by controlling the Strait of Malacca, a critical chokepoint where it taxed passing ships and regulated trade", "New knowledge and tech"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image13.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image13.jpeg", "bytes": 230953, "width": 1600, "height": 1200, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image13.webp"}], "id": "d3:12"}, {"number": 13, "text": ["Trade routes like the Indian Ocean network carried crops that were native to Southeast Asia to other parts of Afro-Eurasia, transforming diets, economies, and agricultural systems.", "Bananas spread westward to Africa, becoming a reliable staple food that supported population growth.", "Sugarcane spread westward towards Europe. Its cultivation influenced the development of future plantation systems that relied on intensive slave labor and later expanded to the Atlantic world", "Oranges (citrus) spread  to the Middle East and Mediterranean, valued as a luxury good for the wealthy and as a healthy food that helped prevent diseases like scurvy", "Environmental Impact"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image14.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image14.webp", "bytes": 22974, "width": 474, "height": 237, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d3-image14.webp"}], "id": "d3:13"}, {"number": 14, "text": ["Between 1200 and 1450, South and Southeast Asia became a crucial crossroads of religion, trade, and environmental exchange.", "Religious diversity shaped political structures, social hierarchies, and monumental temples that still define the region's cultural heritage today.", "The Indian Ocean trade network integrated Southeast Asia into a vast Afro-Eurasian economy.", "Environmental knowledge particularly of monsoon winds made this connectivity possible, while the diffusion of crops (bananas, sugarcane, citrus) from Southeast Asia transformed diets and agricultural systems across Afro-Eurasia.", "Understanding this period helps explain why Southeast Asia today is home to multiple major world religions, a history of maritime trade, agricultural systems shaped by global exchange, and some of the world's most famous religious monuments.", "Why this matters"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d3-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 273622, "width": 1200, "height": 795, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d3-image15.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image15.jpeg", "bytes": 378808, "width": 640, "height": 480, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d3-image15.webp"}], "id": "d3:14"}], "sha256": "22d614409d794a9aa4cc69dd0b60235f0daa5fad432aa26d16bdc686641677bc", "label": "South & Southeast Asia", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d4", "file": "1_2.4_-_Americas.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1&2.4", "State Building in the Americas", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}], "id": "d4:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did American states demonstrate continuity, innovation, and diversity in their political, social, and religious systems?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}], "id": "d4:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["Long before 1200, complex societies laid the foundation for later American states. In Mesoamerica (Central America), the Olmec (1200 BC) andMaya (250-900 CE) developed advanced traditions.", "In the Andes (South America), the Chavín (900 BC) influenced later civilizations.", "These earlier cultures established patterns of governance, religion, and agriculture – including the cultivation of maize (corn) , which would become a staple crop supporting large populations across the Americas.", "By 1200, the Maya Classic period had ended, setting the stage for the rise of the Aztec and Maya city-states in Mesoamerica, and the Inca in the Andes.", "Context"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image2.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image2.jpeg", "bytes": 467017, "width": 1640, "height": 1087, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image2.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 13286, "width": 474, "height": 457, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d4-image3.webp"}], "id": "d4:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 1659675, "width": 1000, "height": 756, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image4.webp"}], "id": "d4:4", "visualDescription": "Mesoamerican map comparing Teotihuacan, Maya, Toltec, and Aztec geographic areas and date ranges."}, {"number": 5, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image5.png", "original": "ppt/media/image5.png", "bytes": 1386189, "width": 1000, "height": 893, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image5.webp"}], "id": "d4:5", "visualDescription": "Map of Inca territory and road system, with stages from CE 1230 to 1525."}, {"number": 6, "text": ["The Aztec Empire rose in Mesoamerica during the 14th and 15th centuries. The empire was decentralized – formed by the Triple Alliance of three city-states, with Tenochtitlán as the dominant capital.", "The Aztec used a tribute system of indirect rule, conquered states kept local rulers but paid tribute in goods, food, and captives. Valuable tribute goods included feathers, cacao, and obsidian. These goods functioned similarly to luxury goods in Afro-Eurasian trade networks.", "The tribute system was efficient but created resentment among conquered peoples, which Spanish conquerors later exploited.", "Aztec Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image6.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image6.jpeg", "bytes": 149109, "width": 1381, "height": 924, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image6.webp"}], "id": "d4:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["The capital, Tenochtitlán, was built on an island in modern day Mexico and housed over 200,000 people.", "The city featured advanced infrastructure including aqueducts (bringing fresh water from the mainland), causeways (connecting the island to the mainland with bridges that could be raised for defense), and chinampas (\"floating gardens\").", "Chinampas were plots of rich soil built on shallow lake beds, allowing multiple harvests per year and feeding hundreds of thousands of people. These innovations allowed the Aztec to support a massive urban population in the middle of a lake.", "Aztec Infrastructure"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image7.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image7.jpeg", "bytes": 267949, "width": 1756, "height": 952, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image7.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image8.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image8.jpeg", "bytes": 71543, "width": 474, "height": 532, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image8.webp"}], "id": "d4:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["The Inca rose in the Andes during the 1400s. The empire was divided into four diverse provinces around the capital, Cusco.", "Unlike the Aztec's decentralized tribute system, the Inca createda centralized bureaucracy with appointed officials governing each province.", "The Inca did not have a written language, and instead usedquipu (a system of knotted strings) for record-keeping. This included tracking census data, tax obligations, and military organization.", "Inca Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image9.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image9.jpeg", "bytes": 393176, "width": 1200, "height": 675, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image9.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image10.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image10.webp", "bytes": 42652, "width": 820, "height": 500, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d4-image10.webp"}], "id": "d4:8"}, {"number": 9, "text": ["The Carpa Nan (Inca road system) stretched over 25,000 miles across the Andes, including roads, bridges, and tunnels. This network allowed rapid military movement and communication. A message could travel over 1,500 kilometers across the empire in about one week.", "The mit'a labor system required all male citizens to perform public service, including road building, farming, and military service. In return, the state provided food, clothing, and care for the sick and elderly.", "The Inca also carved terraced farms into mountainsides to allow for farming and maximize crop yields.", "Inca Infrastructure"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image11.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image11.webp", "bytes": 89630, "width": 820, "height": 500, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d4-image11.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image12.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image12.jpeg", "bytes": 713838, "width": 3840, "height": 2160, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image12.webp"}], "id": "d4:9"}, {"number": 10, "text": ["Religion in the Americas legitimized political authority and unified diverse populations, much like in Afro-Eurasian states.", "Both the Aztec and Inca were polytheistic; they believed in many gods. The sun god was the most important god in both empires. Aztec and Inca rulers both claimed divine authority.", "Human sacrifice was central to Aztec religion. Captives were sacrificed to ensure the sun would rise each day, with thousands sacrificed annually.", "The Inca practiced limited human sacrifice (primarily during crises), instead emphasizing offerings of goods and llamas.", "Native American Religion"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image13.png", "original": "ppt/media/image13.png", "bytes": 1594835, "width": 829, "height": 1018, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image13.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image14.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image14.jpeg", "bytes": 314561, "width": 1461, "height": 1076, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image14.webp"}], "id": "d4:10"}, {"number": 11, "text": ["Unlike the Aztec or Inca, the Maya never unified into a single empire, even during the Postclassic period (c. 900–1500 CE).", "Instead, they organized into decentralized competing city-states. Each city-state was ruled by a divine king who claimed descent from the gods.", "Despite not being politically united, the Maya shared a common culture, religion, and trade networks. The Maya made remarkable intellectual achievements, including the concept of zero, the most accurate calendar of its time, and a complex writing system using heiroglyphs – all achieved without metal tools or the wheel.", "Maya City States"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image15.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image15.jpeg", "bytes": 225304, "width": 1280, "height": 855, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image15.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image16.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image16.webp", "bytes": 28058, "width": 474, "height": 330, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d4-image16.webp"}], "id": "d4:11"}, {"number": 12, "text": ["North America was home to hundreds of diverse native societies, ranging from small nomadic bands to settled agricultural chiefdoms. Unlike the Aztec or Inca, no large empires emerged in North America.", "However, these societies were far from \"simple.”They built large settlements, engaged in long-distance trade, and developed complex social structures.", "Cahokia was the largest settlement north of Mexico.", "At its peak (c. 1050–1200), it housed 10,000–20,000", "people and featured Monks Mound (pictured). Cahokia was a major trade hub – goods from across the continent", "have been found there.", "This demonstrates that North American societies were connected through complex trade networks long before European contact.", "North American Natives"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image17.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image17.webp", "bytes": 12530, "width": 474, "height": 240, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d4-image17.webp"}], "id": "d4:12"}, {"number": 13, "text": ["Between 1200 and 1450, states in the Americas built complex political, social, and religious systems independently of Afro-Eurasia – with no contact, no trade, and no knowledge of civilizations on the other side of the ocean.", "Unlike Afro-Eurasia, where ideas, technologies, and diseases spread constantly along trade routes (Silk Roads, Indian Ocean), American civilizations evolved in isolation. The wheel was never adapted for transport. Ironworking never developed (stone and obsidian remained primary tools). Writing was rare (only the Maya developed a full writing system).", "The arrival of Europeans after 1492 would destroy most of these civilizations through conquest and disease. But understanding American states before contact challenges the outdated myth that \"advanced\" civilizations existed only in Afro-Eurasia. The peoples of the Americas built their own complex world – one that was completely unknown to Europe, Africa, and Asia until the end of this period.", "Why this matters!"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d4-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1343369, "width": 2624, "height": 1726, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d4-image18.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image18.jpeg", "bytes": 1064169, "width": 1920, "height": 1120, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d4-image18.webp"}], "id": "d4:13"}], "sha256": "9206d827383cd335608ba61f9af31f1703e5448b0774be006d8e2272c9d96141", "label": "Americas", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d5", "file": "1_2.5_-_State_Building_in_Africa.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1.5", "State Building in Africa", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}], "id": "d5:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did African states demonstrate continuity, innovation, and diversity while integrating into trans-Saharan and Indian Ocean trade networks?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}], "id": "d5:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["Long before 1200, Africa was home to powerful kingdoms and complex trade networks. In West Africa, the Kingdom of Ghana (c. 300–1200 CE) controlled trans-Saharan trade in gold and salt, becoming wealthy and powerful.", "In East Africa, coastal trading cities had begun connecting African interior goods to Indian Ocean merchants from Arabia, Persia, and India.", "In southern Africa, the ancestors of the Great Zimbabwe civilization were building stone settlements and controlling local gold trade.", "By 1200, Ghana had declined, setting the stage for the rise of Mali in West Africa, the Swahili city-states on the East African coast, and Great Zimbabwe in the south.", "Context: Africa before 1200"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image2.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image2.webp", "bytes": 16026, "width": 474, "height": 474, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image2.webp"}], "id": "d5:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["Diversity characterized these states, with political systems ranging from centralized kingdoms to decentralized chiefdoms.", "Centralized states like Mali (West Africa), Ethiopia (East Africa), and Great Zimbabwe (Southern Africa) developed strong political hierarchies and administrative systems to consolidate power and manage trade.", "Ethiopia was a Christian kingdom surrounded by Islamic states.  a unique example of a non-Islamic centralized state in Africa.", "Decentralized societies like the Igbo in West Africa relied on communal decision-making and kinship family networks to govern effectively, with no kings or emperors.", "African State Systems"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 38350, "width": 474, "height": 661, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image3.webp"}], "id": "d5:4"}, {"number": 5, "text": ["The trans-Saharan trade network linked West Africa to North", "Africa and the Mediterranean, facilitating the exchange of", "gold, salt, slaves, and ivory.", "Innovations in transportation made this trade possible. The", "camel saddle allowed merchants to carry heavier loads and", "travel greater distances across the desert.", "The expansion of empires like Mali facilitated trade by controlling", "key routes and ensuring the flow of goods. Islam spread across", "West Africa through these trade networks.", "Trans-Sahara Trade Route"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 181316, "width": 468, "height": 264, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image4.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image5.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image5.webp", "bytes": 27586, "width": 474, "height": 316, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image5.webp"}], "id": "d5:5"}, {"number": 6, "text": ["The Mali Empire (c. 1230–1600) rose in West Africa after the decline of Ghana, controlling trans-Saharan trade routes and becoming one of the wealthiest empires in the world.", "Mali was a centralized state that managed trade and taxation across its vast territories.", "The empire controlled the production and trade of gold, salt, slaves, and ivory –goods in high demand across North Africa and the Mediterranean.", "Mali's most famous ruler, Mansa Musa , made a legendary pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324–1325. His pilgrimage demonstrated Mali's immense wealth and established diplomatic and economic ties with the Islamic world.", "Mali's rulers combined political authority with religious leadership, using Islam to legitimize their rule. Cities like Timbuktu and Gao became major trading hubs and centers of Islamic learning, attracting scholars and merchants from across Afro-Eurasia.", "West Africa: Mali"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image6.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image6.jpeg", "bytes": 173252, "width": 1024, "height": 768, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image6.webp"}], "id": "d5:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["On the East African coast, a network of Swahili city-states (such as Kilwa, Mogadishu, Mombasa, and Zanzibar) flourished from 1200 to 1450.", "These city-states were not unified into a single empire. Instead, they were decentralized. Each was an independent trading port that controlled a section of the coast.", "The Swahili city-states grew wealthy by connecting African interior goods (gold, ivory, slaves, timber) with Indian Ocean trade networks reaching Arabia, Persia, India, and China.", "The Swahili language developed as a blend of Bantu (African) and Arabic, reflecting centuries of syncretism.", "These city-states adopted Islam and used Islamic governance structures,but they blended them with local African traditions. Rulers built stone palaces and mosques (such as Kilwa's Great Mosque) using Swahili architectural style.", "East Africa - Swahili States"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image7.png", "original": "ppt/media/image7.png", "bytes": 1007085, "width": 816, "height": 1168, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image7.webp"}], "id": "d5:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["Along the Indian Ocean trade routes, merchants established diasporic communities,  permanent settlements of foreign merchants living far from their homeland.", "Arab and Persian merchants settled in East African port cities, introducing Islam, Arabic language, and new architectural styles. These merchants often married into local African families, creating syncreticblended families and cultures.", "These diasporic communities served as intermediaries in trade, connecting different cultural and economic systems. They introduced their own cultural practices, religions, and food to local populations while also taking on elements of indigenous cultures", "Diasporic communities"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image8.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image8.webp", "bytes": 29372, "width": 474, "height": 315, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image8.webp"}], "id": "d5:8"}, {"number": 9, "text": ["In southern Africa, the city of Great Zimbabwe (c. 1000–1450) became the capital of a powerful kingdom controlling local gold trade.", "The city is famous for its massive stone structures. These walls were built without mortar, a technique that required skilled labor and centralized planning. The outer wall stands over 30 feet tall and stretches nearly 800 feet, making it the largest structure in sub-Saharan Africa during this time period.", "These stone walls served multiple purposes: they demonstrated the wealth and authority of the ruling elite, and provided defense from invaders.", "Great Zimbabwe controlled key trade routes connecting the African interior to the Swahili coast. The kingdom exported gold, ivory, and copper to Swahili city-states which then shipped these goods across the Indian Ocean.", "South Africa - Zimbabwe"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image9.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image9.webp", "bytes": 22832, "width": 474, "height": 268, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image9.webp"}], "id": "d5:9"}, {"number": 10, "text": ["Between 1200 and 1450, Africa was fully integrated into Dar al-Islam – the Islamic world stretching from West Africa to Southeast Asia. West African empires like Mali and the Swahili city-states of East Africa adopted Islam, built mosques, established Islamic schools, and connected to broader Muslim trade networks.", "Mansa Musa's pilgrimage to Mecca (1324–1325) demonstrated that African Muslims were not small players in Islam. They were among its wealthiest and most powerful practicers. Timbuktu became a center of Islamic learning, attracting scholars from across Dar al-Islam.", "The same trade routes that brought Islam to Africa alsocarried African gold, ivory, and slaves to the rest of the Islamic world. After 1450, Europeans would arrive seeking the same wealth, eventually transforming Africa's relationship with the world from equal partner to exploited colony. But before that, Africa was not a \"dark continent\" – it was a core part of the Islamic world.", "Why this matters!"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d5-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 16303170, "width": 5472, "height": 3648, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d5-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d5-image10.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image10.webp", "bytes": 16654, "width": 474, "height": 316, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d5-image10.webp"}], "id": "d5:10"}], "sha256": "52dc0b06afea792de5adc75369a19310de6c24ad65e4aa8813b41b42b03a0888", "label": "Africa", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d6", "file": "1_2.6_-_Developments_in_Europe.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1&2.6", "Developments in Europe", "from c. 1200 to c. 1450", "AP World History – Unit 1"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}], "id": "d6:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did Europe remain politically fragmented while maintaining religious and cultural unity through Christianity?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}], "id": "d6:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["Long before 1200, Europe had already experienced the rise and fall of centralized authority. The Roman Empire (27 BCE – 476 CE) had unified the Mediterranean world but collapsed under internal decay and external invasions.", "Following Rome's fall, the Byzantine Empire continued Roman traditions in the East, with its capital at Constantinople. In Western Europe, Europe was politically decentralized, with local lords controlling land and resources.", "The Great Schism of 1054 formally split Christianity into the Roman Catholic Church (West) and the Eastern Orthodox Church (East).", "The Crusades (beginning 1096) brought Western Europeans into contact with the Islamic world, introducing new ideas, technologies, and trade goods.", "Context (pre-1200)"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image2.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image2.webp", "bytes": 43290, "width": 474, "height": 355, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image2.webp"}], "id": "d6:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["Unlike the centralized empires of China, Dar al-Islam, or the Inca, Western Europe was politically decentralized – power was local.", "Feudalism organized European society into a hierarchy of mutual obligations. Nobles owned land (fiefs) and granted it to vassals (lesser lords) in exchange for military service and loyalty. Knights provided protection, and peasants (serfs) worked the land in exchange for safety and a place to live.", "The Catholic Church served as the one unifying institution across Europe,providing moral authority, education, and social services, but even the Church was decentralized with local churches holding authority over their land.", "Kings struggled to centralize power because nobles and the Church often had more influence over local politics than the king. The Magna Carta in England showed these limits. It granted rights to nobles and established that the king was subject to the law, not above it.", "Feudalism"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 15794, "width": 474, "height": 266, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image3.webp"}], "id": "d6:4"}, {"number": 5, "text": ["The manorial system was the economic engine of feudal Europe. A manor was a self-sufficient estate where peasants (serfs) worked the land for their lord.", "Serfdom was a form of coerced labor. Serfs were not slaves (they could not be bought or sold individually), but they were bound to the land meaning they could not leave without their lord's permission and owed their lord a set number of labor days each week.", "Agricultural innovations increased productivity and supported population growth. The heavy plow allowed farmers to till the thick, wet soils of Northern Europe. The three-field system rotated crops (one field planted in fall, one in spring, one left without crops) to prevent overfarming, increasing yields.", "The manorial system made Europe largely self-sufficient. Most goods were produced locally rather than traded over long distances, unlike the commercialized economy of Song China or the trade networks of the Indian Ocean.", "The Manorial System"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image4.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image4.webp", "bytes": 33702, "width": 474, "height": 346, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image4.webp"}], "id": "d6:5"}, {"number": 6, "text": ["Despite political fragmentation, the Catholic Church unified Western Europe through shared Christian  beliefs and Latin as a common language of learning. The pope was the spiritual leader of the Church, and the Scholastic movement fostered education by combining Christian education with Roman classics. Gothic architecture (like Notre-Dame in Paris) reflected the Church's wealth and influence.", "Jewish communities contributed to European society through trade and finance, often serving as moneylenders (Christians were forbidden from this). However, they faced discriminationand massacres (especially during the Crusades).", "In Spain (al-Andalus in 1200s), Muslims, Christians, and Jews lived together and created a flourishing of art, science, and philosophy. However in the late 1400s, the Reconquista (centuries-long Christian re-conquest) gradually pushed out Muslims and Jews reunifying a Christian Spain.", "Religion in Europe"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image5.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image5.webp", "bytes": 21916, "width": 474, "height": 300, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image5.webp"}], "id": "d6:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["The Black Death (Mid 1300s) was one of the deadliest pandemics in human history, killing an estimated one-third of Europe's population.", "Impacts of the plague:", "Labor shortages – With so many people dead, surviving workers could demand higher wages and better conditions. This weakened the manorial system and serfdom, as peasants left their manors for higher-paying work in towns.", "Peasant revolts – The English Peasants' Revolt (1381) and similar uprisings across Europe demonstrated growing discontent with feudalhierarchies.", "Decline of Church authority – The plague killed priests and bishops, and the Church's inability to explain or stop the plague led some to question its authority.", "Shift toward centralized monarchies – The Black Death and the Hundred Years' War between England and France weakened feudal structures. In their aftermath, both kingdoms began centralizing power, setting the stage for the emergence of more modern nation-states.", "The Black Death"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image6.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image6.webp", "bytes": 14310, "width": 474, "height": 213, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image6.webp"}], "id": "d6:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["Between 1200 and 1450, Europe was isolated compared to connected civilizations of China and the Islamic world. While Song China had a commercialized economy, paper money, and gunpowder, and Dar al-Islam stretched from West Africa to Southeast Asia, connecting continents through trade and scholarship, Europe remained politically fragmented, rural, and isolated.", "European kingdoms were decentralized. The economy was based on manorialism and serfdom, not long-distance trade. Most Europeans never left their village. In contrast, Muslim merchants traveled from Spain to India, and Chinese junks sailed the Indian Ocean.", "But Europe was not completely cut off. Contact with the Islamic world through the Crusades would bring Greek philosophy, Islamic science, and technologies to Europe.", "By 1450, Europe would transform. The Renaissance (beginning in Italy) revived classical learning and art. Within decades, Europeans – using technologies borrowed from China (compass, gunpowder) and the Islamic world (astrolabe) – would launch voyages across the Atlantic, ending Europe's isolation.", "Why this matters"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d6-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 207046, "width": 842, "height": 509, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d6-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d6-image7.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image7.webp", "bytes": 40616, "width": 474, "height": 348, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d6-image7.webp"}], "id": "d6:8"}], "sha256": "ac2174f4b0943354d981506fc3e288241db611a0c833e60800f03ef055d21be7", "label": "Europe", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d7", "file": "1_2.7_-_The_Mongol_Empire.pptx", "slides": [{"number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 1&2.7", "The Mongol Empire and", "the Making of the Modern World", "AP World History – Unit 1&2"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}], "id": "d7:1"}, {"number": 2, "text": ["How did the Mongols conquer and create the largest land empire in history while facilitating trade, communication, and cultural exchange across Afro-Eurasia?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}], "id": "d7:2"}, {"number": 3, "text": ["The Mongols were a collection of nomadic tribes living on the steppes of Central Asia. In 1206, a single ruler united the Mongol tribes and took the title Genghis Khan (\"Universal Ruler\").", "Under Genghis Khan and his successors, the Mongols conquered the largest land empire in history. By 1294, the empire had split into four main khanates:", "Yuan Dynasty (China) – ruled by Kublai Khan", "Ilkhanate (Persia/Middle East) -", "Golden Horde (Russia/Eastern Europe)", "Chagatai Khanate (Central Asia)", "Context"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d7-image2.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image2.webp", "bytes": 19912, "width": 474, "height": 274, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d7-image2.webp"}], "id": "d7:3"}, {"number": 4, "text": ["Mongol", "Khanates", "in 1294"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image3.png", "original": "ppt/media/image3.png", "bytes": 2718032, "width": 1492, "height": 1094, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image3.webp"}], "id": "d7:4", "visualDescription": "1294 Mongol map labeling Golden Horde, Chagatai, Ilkhanate, and Great Khan territories, Silk Roads, and the Great Wall."}, {"number": 5, "text": ["The Mongols mastered psychological warfare. Before attacking a city, they would demand surrender. If the city surrendered without resistance, the people were usually spared. But if they resisted, the Mongols would massacre everyone,men, women, and children, as a warning to others.", "This reputation for brutality often preceded them, causing cities to surrender without a fight. For example, when the Mongols conquered Baghdad in 1258, they killed tens of thousands, sending a clear message to the rest of the Islamic world.", "However, the Mongols were also smart rulers. They had no experience governing cities or empires they were nomadic herders. So they employed foreign officials to administer their conquered territories. They adopted local technologies (like gunpowder from China), local administrative practices (like Persian bureaucracy), and tolerated all religions. This allowed conquered peoples to maintain their customs as long as they submitted to the Khan (king).", "Mongol Tactics"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d7-image4.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image4.webp", "bytes": 39344, "width": 474, "height": 347, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d7-image4.webp"}], "id": "d7:5"}, {"number": 6, "text": ["The Mongols brought stability to the Silk Roads, creating a period known as the Pax Mongolica (\"Mongol Peace\").", "Unlike the fragmented and dangerous trade routes before them, the Mongols controlled the entire length of the Silk Roads from China to Persia to Russia. They secured the roads making long-distance trade safer and cheaper than ever before.", "The Mongols built infrastructure to support trade and communication such as the Yam system: a network of relay stations with fresh horses and supplies, allowing messengers to travel rapidly across the empire", "Merchants, travelers, and diplomats moved freely across the empire. For example, from European Venice, the merchant Marco Polo traveled to China and served in Kublai Khan's court. His writings introduced Europeans to the wealth and power of Asia.", "Pax Mongolica"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d7-image5.png", "original": "ppt/media/image5.png", "bytes": 2537977, "width": 851, "height": 1176, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image5.webp"}], "id": "d7:6"}, {"number": 7, "text": ["Because of the Pax Mongolica, technologies, ideas, religions, and diseases spread easily across Afro-Eurasia. The same trade routes that carried goods also carried knowledge.", "Technologies – Gunpowder spread from China to the Middle East and Europe transforming warfare. Paper and printing moved westward, revolutionizing how information was shared. The compass allowed sailors to navigate open oceans, eventually enabling European exploration.", "Ideas and religions – Buddhism flourished in Persia under Mongol rule. Islam expanded into Central Asia and China. Christianity also traveled along the Silk Roads to many regions.", "Disease – The Black Death spread from Central Asia along Mongol trade routes, reaching the Black Sea in the 1340s and then Europe in 1347", "The Mongols themselves did not intend to spread these things, but by creating a single, connected empire across Eurasia, they made it possible.", "Cultural diffusion"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d7-image6.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image6.jpeg", "bytes": 52602, "width": 432, "height": 295, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image6.webp"}], "id": "d7:7"}, {"number": 8, "text": ["By the mid-1300s, the Mongol Empire began to fall apart. The khanates fought each other, and local rebellions grew stronger. The Mongols had relied on their military superiority, but as conquered peoples adapted Mongol tactics and adopted gunpowder weapons, Mongol power declined.", "The Yuan Dynasty fell to the Ming Dynasty (1368). The Ming restored native Chinese rule, erasing many Mongol symbols while keeping some Mongol administrative practices.", "The Golden Horde ruled Russia for over 200 years. But as the Mongols weakened, the Russian principality of Moscow grew stronger. Ivan III (Ivan the Great) stopped paying tribute in 1480, marking the end of Mongol rule. Russia would later become a centralized state.", "The spread of gunpowder enabled the rise of gunpowder empires after 1450: the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal Empires. These empires used cannons and firearms to conquer and centralize power, replacing the fragmented Mongol khanates with new, more stable states.", "Fall of the Mongols"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d7-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1749527, "width": 2424, "height": 1651, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d7-image7.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image7.jpeg", "bytes": 531607, "width": 1280, "height": 766, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d7-image7.webp"}], "id": "d7:8"}], "sha256": "b45c429a8406303d3738267ea768430e413d03bfce342583c5d11b0692fbd843", "label": "Mongol Empire", "unit": "units12"}, {"id": "d8", "label": "3.1 · Land-Based Empires", "file": "3.1_-_Landbased_Empires.pptx", "sha256": "fae57a384c1ab2a199ad3b5cfd1cd2feb7c72184a9d13f054a1fe8232626003c", "unit": "unit3", "slides": [{"id": "d8:1", "number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 3.1", "Landbased Empires", "AP World History – Unit 3"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:2", "number": 2, "text": ["How and why did land-based empires develop and expand between 1450 and 1750?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:3", "number": 3, "text": ["Between 1450 and 1750, powerful land-based empires expanded across Africa and Eurasia. These empires conquered neighboring territories, incorporated diverse populations, and competed for control of valuable land and resources.", "One important factor behind their expansion was the increasing use of gunpowder weapons. Cannons and firearms helped rulers defeat enemies, capture fortified cities, and establish control over large territories.", "The Ottoman, Safavid, Mughal, and Qing empires became major powers during this period. Although their histories differed, each expanded through military conquest and competition with neighboring states.", "Empires Expand"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 1050010, "width": 808, "height": 698, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image2.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:4", "number": 4, "text": ["Gunpowder was originally developed in China and spread across Eurasia through trade and warfare. By the 1400s, improvements in cannons and firearms were transforming military strategies.", "Cannons were especially effective against fortified cities and defensive walls, while firearms increased the destructive power of armies. States that successfully incorporated these weapons could gain advantages over their rivals.", "The term gunpowder empires is often used to describe powerful states such as the Ottoman, Safavid, and Mughal empires. However, military technology alone did not guarantee victory. Effective leadership, organization, and resources remained essential.", "Gunpowder Empires"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image3.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image3.webp", "bytes": 25624, "width": 474, "height": 316, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d8-image3.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:5", "number": 5, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 2387211, "width": 1780, "height": 1247, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image4.webp"}], "visualDescription": "Image-only map showing the Safavid Empire in Persia beside the Ottoman Empire, with areas of overlap marked.", "visualTitle": "Map · Safavid and Ottoman territories"}, {"id": "d8:6", "number": 6, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image5.png", "original": "ppt/media/image5.png", "bytes": 2536037, "width": 2382, "height": 1690, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image5.webp"}], "visualDescription": "Image-only map of Ottoman expansion. The legend distinguishes acquisitions from 1300 through 1683 and the boundary at its greatest extent in 1683–1699.", "visualTitle": "Map · Ottoman expansion"}, {"id": "d8:7", "number": 7, "text": [], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image6.png", "original": "ppt/media/image6.png", "bytes": 1543050, "width": 1038, "height": 1038, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image6.webp"}], "visualDescription": "Image-only map titled Growth of the Mughal Empire, 1526–1707. The Babur legend says 1525, while other areas are labeled as added by Akbar by 1605 and Aurangzeb by 1707. The conflicting first dates are preserved as a source caution.", "visualTitle": "Map · Mughal expansion"}, {"id": "d8:8", "number": 8, "text": ["The Ottoman Empire emerged in Anatolia (modern Turkey) and expanded into southeastern Europe, the Middle East, and North Africa. Its strategic position allowed Ottoman rulers to compete for important territories and trade routes.", "In 1453, Sultan Mehmed II conquered Constantinople, the capital of the Byzantine Empire. Ottoman forces used powerful cannons to attack the city's defensive walls, ending the Byzantine Empire and establishing Constantinople, renamed Istanbul, as a major Ottoman capital.", "The Ottomans followed Sunni Islam, the largest branch of Islam, which traditionally recognized the legitimacy of leaders chosen by the early Muslim community after Muhammad's death. This differed from Shia Islam, whose followers believed leadership rightfully belonged to Muhammad's family through Ali.", "Under Suleiman the Magnificent (1520–1566), the Ottomans expanded further into Europe and the Middle East, becoming one of the most powerful empires of the early modern world.", "Ottoman Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image7.png", "original": "ppt/media/image7.png", "bytes": 476327, "width": 812, "height": 674, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image7.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:9", "number": 9, "text": ["The Safavid Empire emerged in Persia in 1501 under Shah Ismail I. Through military conquest, the Safavids established control over much of Persia and competed with neighboring powers.", "Unlike the Sunni Ottoman Empire, the Safavids established Twelver Shia Islam as their official religion. Shia Muslims believed that legitimate religious leadership descended from Muhammad through Ali and his descendants. This religious distinction became an important part of Safavid identity.", "Religious differences reinforced political and territorial rivalries between the Ottoman and Safavid empires. Both sought control over strategically important territories in the Middle East.", "In 1514, Ottoman and Safavid armies fought at the Battle of Chaldiran. Ottoman firearms and artillery contributed to their victory, demonstrating the military importance of gunpowder technology. Their rivalry continued for generations.", "Safavid Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 2387211, "width": 1780, "height": 1247, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image4.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:10", "number": 10, "text": ["The Mughal Empire was established in northern India by", "Babur, a Central Asian ruler descended from Genghis Khan.", "Babur sought to conquer territory in the wealthy Indian", "subcontinent.", "Babur defeated the Delhi Sultanate. His army used artillery", "and firearms alongside cavalry and effective battlefield tactics.", "This victory marked the beginning of Mughal rule in India.", "Later Mughal rulers, including Akbar the Great, expanded the empire across much of South Asia. Their conquests brought large populations and diverse religious communities under Mughal rule.", "Mughal Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image6.png", "original": "ppt/media/image6.png", "bytes": 1543050, "width": 1038, "height": 1038, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image6.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:11", "number": 11, "text": ["In East Asia, the Manchus emerged as a powerful military force northeast of China. They developed strong cavalry armies and incorporated gunpowder weapons as they challenged the declining Ming Dynasty.", "In 1644, Manchu forces entered Beijing and established the Qing Dynasty. Qing rulers gradually consolidated control over China and continued expanding into neighboring regions.", "Under emperors such as Qianlong, the Qing expanded into areas including Mongolia, Tibet, and Xinjiang. Their conquests created one of the largest territorial empires in Chinese history.", "Qing Dynasty"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image8.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image8.jpeg", "bytes": 125712, "width": 1200, "height": 800, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image8.webp"}], "visualDescription": "The embedded Qing map is captioned circa 1820, later than the 1450–1750 study window. It illustrates a later territorial extent rather than a single snapshot for the whole lesson."}, {"id": "d8:12", "number": 12, "text": ["Following the decline of the Mali Empire in the 1400s, the Songhai Empire emerged as the dominant power in West Africa. Songhai expanded along the Niger River and controlled important trans-Saharan trade routes, including the wealthy cities of Gao and Timbuktu. Like Mali before it, Songhai benefited from the gold and salt trade and supported Islamic scholarship.", "In 1591, North African Morocco invaded Songhai to gain control of its wealth and trade routes. Moroccan forces possessed firearms and artillery, while Songhai relied more heavily on cavalry and traditional weapons. At the Battle of Tondibi, Morocco's gunpowder weapons helped overcome Songhai's numerical advantage.", "The defeat weakened Songhai's central authority and contributed to the empire's fragmentation. Songhai demonstrates how limited access to effective gunpowder weaponry could leave an empire vulnerable to better-equipped rivals.", "Songhai Empire"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d8-image9.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image9.webp", "bytes": 12816, "width": 474, "height": 474, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d8-image9.webp"}]}, {"id": "d8:13", "number": 13, "text": ["Between 1450 and 1750, gunpowder weapons helped transform warfare and contributed to the expansion of powerful land-based empires. The Ottoman, Safavid, Mughal, and Qing empires established control over enormous territories and diverse populations.", "However, expansion depended on more than technology. Military organization, political leadership, economic resources, and competition between states all influenced imperial success.", "These expanding empires reshaped political boundaries across Afro-Eurasia. Their growth also created a new challenge: How could rulers maintain control over such large and diverse populations?", "Why this matters"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d8-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 767154, "width": 2000, "height": 1198, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d8-image1.webp"}]}]}, {"id": "d9", "label": "3.2 · Empire Administration", "file": "3.2_-_Empire_Administration.pptx", "sha256": "ef5103a25b9938e70bffbd24783ee9a7a2ad546ab0619203b5e618739d3ca5cd", "unit": "unit3", "slides": [{"id": "d9:1", "number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 3.2", "Empire Administration", "AP World History – Unit 3"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:2", "number": 2, "text": ["How did rulers legitimize and consolidate their power in land-based empires between 1450 and 1750?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:3", "number": 3, "text": ["As land-based empires expanded between 1450 and 1750, rulers faced the challenge of governing enormous territories with diverse populations. Conquering territory was only the beginning; maintaining control required effective institutions and reliable sources of authority.", "Rulers sought to consolidate power by strengthening central governments, recruiting loyal officials, and developing professional military forces. These institutions helped rulers enforce laws, maintain order, and limit the independence of powerful regional leaders.", "They also needed to legitimize their rule, convincing subjects that their authority was rightful and deserved obedience. Religion, art, and monumental architecture helped accomplish this, while taxes and tribute provided the resources needed to govern.", "Governing Landbased Empires"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 579193, "width": 484, "height": 620, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image2.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:4", "number": 4, "text": ["As empires grew, rulers increasingly relied on bureaucracies, organized systems of officials responsible for carrying out government policies. Bureaucrats collected information, enforced laws, supervised provinces, and managed state resources.", "Rulers also developed professional military forces that served the central government. Unlike armies assembled temporarily by regional nobles, these forces could provide rulers with more dependable military support.", "By recruiting and training loyal administrators and soldiers, rulers strengthened centralized authority and reduced their dependence on powerful local elites. The Ottoman Empire and Tokugawa Japan developed important examples of these systems.", "Bureaucracies and Military Professionals"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image3.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image3.jpeg", "bytes": 65519, "width": 1024, "height": 768, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image3.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:5", "number": 5, "text": ["The Ottoman Empire developed the devshirme system, which recruited Christian boys, primarily from the Balkans, into imperial service. These boys were converted to Islam and educated or trained to serve the Ottoman government.", "Some recruits entered the imperial bureaucracy, where they could become influential administrators. Others joined the Janissaries, an elite infantry force trained to fight for the sultan using firearms and disciplined military tactics.", "Because these officials and soldiers were trained within the imperial system, the sultan could rely on their loyalty and reduce reliance on hereditary nobles. The devshirme helped the Ottomans maintain centralized control over their extensive empire.", "The Ottoman Devshirme System"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 836255, "width": 546, "height": 744, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image4.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:6", "number": 6, "text": ["After generations of civil warfare, the Tokugawa shogunate in 1603 began a period of greater political stability in Japan. The shogun held effective political authority, while the emperor was more ceremonial.", "Japan remained divided into domains governed by powerful regional lords known as daimyo. The Tokugawa shogunate maintained control through regulations and obligations imposed on these lords.", "As warfare declined, many samurai transitioned from warriors into salaried retainers and administrative officials. Their service helped maintain order and support the authority of the shogun and daimyo.", "Japan - Tokugawa Shogunate"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image5.png", "original": "ppt/media/image5.png", "bytes": 931535, "width": 504, "height": 742, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image5.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:7", "number": 7, "text": ["Throughout history, rulers used religion to legitimize their authority, claiming that their right to govern came from divine powers. These beliefs encouraged obedience by presenting political leadership as part of a sacred order.", "In Europe, some monarchs practiced absolutism, a system in which rulers sought to concentrate political authority in the monarchy and limit the independence of powerful nobles and other institutions.", "Louis XIV of France became a prominent example of absolutism. He promoted the divine right of kings, the belief that monarchs received their authority directly from God. This helped justify his efforts to strengthen royal authority and discourage challenges to his rule.", "In the Inca Empire, the Sapa Inca was considered a descendant of Inti, the sun god. This sacred ancestry reinforced the emperor's authority and connected political leadership to Incan religious beliefs.", "Although their religious traditions differed, both rulers used divine authority to legitimize their power.", "Divine Authority"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image6.png", "original": "ppt/media/image6.png", "bytes": 721640, "width": 516, "height": 652, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image6.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:8", "number": 8, "text": ["As land-based empires expanded, rulers governed populations with different religious beliefs. Some rulers practiced religious toleration, allowing subjects to maintain their faith and traditions. These policies helped reduce religious tensions and maintain political stability.", "In the Ottoman Empire, the millet system allowed recognized religious communities, particularly Christians and Jews, to manage many of their own religious and legal affairs. Community leaders oversaw matters such as marriage, education, and religious practices while remaining under Ottoman authority.", "Most Islamic empires, also collected the jizya, a special tax imposed on non-Muslim subjects, particularly Christians and Jews. In exchange, these communities generally received protection and permission to practice their religions, although they did not enjoy the same legal status as Muslims.", "In the Mughal Empire, Emperor Akbar the Great promoted greater religious toleration toward his predominantly Hindu population. In 1564, he abolished the jizya tax and welcomed Hindus into government positions. These policies helped strengthen cooperation and loyalty among the empire's diverse religious communities.", "Religious Toleration"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image7.webp", "original": "ppt/media/image7.webp", "bytes": 69000, "width": 474, "height": 603, "format": "WEBP", "display": "assets/web/d9-image7.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:9", "number": 9, "text": ["Rulers also commissioned impressive artwork and monumental buildings to demonstrate their wealth, power, and legitimacy. Palaces, temples, portraits, and religious monuments made imperial authority visible to subjects and foreign visitors.", "In France, Louis XIV built the Palace of Versailles, an enormous royal residence. Its elaborate architecture displayed royal wealth, while court life at Versailles helped the king supervise and influence powerful nobles.", "In China, Qing imperial portraits presented emperors as dignified and legitimate rulers.", "In the Inca Empire, the Temple of the Sun at Cuzco reinforced the sacred authority of the Sapa Inca, who was associated with the sun god Inti.", "In Mughal India, rulers created magnificent mosques and mausoleums. The Taj Mahal demonstrated imperial wealth and artistic achievement", "Art and Architecture"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image8.png", "original": "ppt/media/image8.png", "bytes": 1773653, "width": 1190, "height": 748, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image8.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:10", "number": 10, "text": ["As land-based empires expanded, rulers needed reliable sources of revenue to support their governments, maintain armies, and fund public projects. Taxes and tribute provided these resources. For example, the Mexica (Aztec) Empire required conquered peoples to provide tribute in the form of goods, agricultural products, and other valuable resources.", "In the Ottoman Empire, rulers used tax farming, a system in which individuals purchased the right to collect taxes in a particular region. Tax farmers paid the government an agreed amount and could keep additional revenue as profit. This allowed the Ottoman government to collect revenue without directly managing every taxpayer.", "In the Mughal Empire, zamindars were local landholders and elites who helped collect agricultural taxes from farmers. They transferred revenue to the imperial government while retaining a portion for themselves. By relying on established local authorities, Mughal rulers could collect taxes across their extensive territories.", "In Ming China, the government increasingly required taxes to be paid in silver rather than goods or labor. This helped simplify taxation and increased demand for silver, connecting China's economy more closely to expanding global trade networks.", "Taxation"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d9-image9.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image9.jpeg", "bytes": 78390, "width": 603, "height": 783, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image9.webp"}]}, {"id": "d9:11", "number": 11, "text": ["Between 1450 and 1750, land-based empires developed a variety of methods to maintain control over their territories. Bureaucracies and military professionals helped rulers enforce their authority and consolidate power, while taxation and tribute supplied the resources necessary to support imperial governments.", "Rulers also used religion, art, and monumental architecture to legitimize their power. These practices encouraged obedience, reinforced political hierarchies, and demonstrated the wealth and prestige of ruling dynasties.", "Although different empires developed different systems, they faced similar challenges: controlling regional elites, securing revenue, and maintaining the loyalty of diverse populations. Imperial expansion required military strength, but imperial survival depended on effective administration.", "Why this matters!"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d9-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 220977, "width": 1361, "height": 926, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d9-image1.webp"}]}]}, {"id": "d10", "label": "3.3 · Empire Belief Systems", "file": "3.3_-_Empire_Belief_Systems.pptx", "sha256": "4a8ca29ab610a593d5a58f6e7d19f0a43ec1941783a0de8577beebab697e2ce2", "unit": "unit3", "slides": [{"id": "d10:1", "number": 1, "text": ["PowerPoint 3.3", "Empire Belief Systems", "AP World History – Unit 3"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d10:2", "number": 2, "text": ["How did religious beliefs change and develop between 1450 and 1750, and how did political rivalries and cultural interactions influence these changes?", "Driving Question"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}]}, {"id": "d10:3", "number": 3, "text": ["During the early sixteenth century, the Roman Catholic Church was the dominant religious institution in Western Europe. However, growing criticism of Church corruption and religious practices challenged its authority. One controversial practice was the sale of indulgences, which were associated with reducing punishment for sins.", "In 1517, German monk Martin Luther published his Ninety-Five Theses, criticizing the sale of indulgences. Luther argued that salvation came through faith alone, rather than through good works or payments to the Church. He also taught that the Bible, rather than the pope, should be the highest religious authority.", "Luther's ideas spread rapidly through the printing press, encouraging the growth of Protestant Christianity. New denominations, including Lutheranism and Calvinism, challenged Catholic authority. Some European rulers supported Protestant movements to gain greater independence from the Catholic Church.", "The Protestant Reformation permanently divided Western Christianity and contributed to religious and political conflicts across Europe.", "The Protestant Reformation"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d10-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 551630, "width": 531, "height": 765, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image2.webp"}]}, {"id": "d10:4", "number": 4, "text": ["The Catholic Church responded to the Protestant Reformation through a movement known as the Catholic Reformation, or Counter-Reformation. Church leaders sought to correct abuses, strengthen religious discipline, and reaffirm Catholic beliefs.", "At the Council of Trent (1545–1563), Catholic leaders reaffirmed the authority of the Church, the importance of faith and good works, and traditional Catholic teachings. They also introduced reforms to improve the education and conduct of priests.", "The Catholic Church also expanded its missionary activities. The Jesuits, a Catholic religious order, established schools and sent missionaries throughout the world. Missionaries helped spread Catholic Christianity into parts of Asia, while Spanish and Portuguese colonial expansion contributed to the growth of Christianity in the Americas.", "Although the Reformation divided European Christianity, both Protestant and Catholic movements contributed to Christianity's continued expansion during this period.", "The Catholic Counter Reformation"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d10-image2.png", "original": "ppt/media/image2.png", "bytes": 551630, "width": 531, "height": 765, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image2.webp"}]}, {"id": "d10:5", "number": 5, "text": ["In South Asia, centuries of interaction between Hindu and Muslim communities created an environment in which new religious ideas could develop. During the late fifteenth century, Guru Nanak founded Sikhism in northern India.", "Sikhism emerged in a society influenced by both Hinduism and Islam, but developed into a distinct religious tradition. Guru Nanak taught belief in one God, the spiritual equality of all people, and the importance of honest work, service, and devotion. Sikh teachings challenged caste discrimination and emphasized that individuals could develop a direct relationship with God.", "Over time, a succession of Sikh gurus helped organize and expand the religious community. Under Mughal rule, Sikh communities sometimes experienced conflict with imperial authorities, contributing to their growing political and military organization.", "The Golden Temple in Amritsar, which developed into Sikhism's most important religious center, symbolizes the lasting influence of this new religious tradition.", "Development of Sikhism"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d10-image3.png", "original": "ppt/media/image3.png", "bytes": 1389740, "width": 966, "height": 723, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image3.webp"}]}, {"id": "d10:6", "number": 6, "text": ["Between 1450 and 1750, religious traditions continued to influence societies and political authority, but they also underwent significant transformations. The Protestant Reformation divided Western Christianity, while the Catholic Reformation strengthened Catholic institutions and expanded Christianity around the world. In the Islamic world, Ottoman–Safavid political competition intensified existing Sunni–Shi'a divisions. In South Asia, interactions between religious traditions contributed to the emergence of Sikhism.", "These developments reveal that religion was not simply a source of unity. It could also create political rivalries, challenge established authority, and inspire new communities. Many of the religious divisions and traditions that developed or intensified during this period continue to influence societies today.", "Why this matters!"], "notes": [], "images": [{"path": "assets/slides/d10-image1.jpeg", "original": "ppt/media/image1.jpeg", "bytes": 1642450, "width": 5300, "height": 2409, "format": "JPEG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image1.webp"}, {"path": "assets/slides/d10-image4.png", "original": "ppt/media/image4.png", "bytes": 1596020, "width": 1031, "height": 818, "format": "PNG", "display": "assets/web/d10-image4.webp"}]}]}];